{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/c1-explainer-about-grok-bot-new-developments-in-ai-anthropic-j-space-claude-and-other-llm/audio/main-6eff720547c7.mp3","durationSec":376.68,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":5.1,"text":"AI news often treats *models*, *chatbots*, and *agents* as if they were identical."},{"start":5.98,"end":10.7,"text":"A large language model, or LLM, produces and interprets language."},{"start":11.6,"end":15.3,"text":"An agent uses a model as part of a larger system that can take actions."},{"start":16.26,"end":21.54,"text":"To examine such systems, we can use three questions: What model supports the system?"},{"start":21.96,"end":23.16,"text":"What tools can it use?"},{"start":23.88,"end":25.44,"text":"When must a human intervene?"},{"start":25.44,"end":32.94,"text":"Grok Bot, introduced in early beta on 11 August 2026, is a team of always-on agents."},{"start":33.74,"end":37.54,"text":"Each agent has its own cloud computer and can sign into tools and apps."},{"start":38.16,"end":45.76,"text":"It can work across websites, including sites without an API—a formal connection that allows software systems to communicate directly."},{"start":46.52,"end":52.12,"text":"The agents are intended to complete jobs from beginning to end and return to the user when approval is needed."},{"start":52.62,"end":55.56,"text":"Users can message Grok Bot from desktop or iOS."},{"start":56.06,"end":62.56,"text":"The announced beta access included several named subscription tiers, while enterprise access remained on a waitlist."},{"start":63.94,"end":73.16,"text":"These details show why “agent” is the more accurate term: the product does not merely generate an answer; it operates within a controlled digital environment."},{"start":74.28,"end":79.84,"text":"The model supporting an agent matters because an extended task requires more than one fluent response."},{"start":80.52,"end":87.92,"text":"The system may need to preserve context, react to changing information, use tools, and continue working through several stages."},{"start":89,"end":96.66,"text":"Grok 4.6, announced on 12 August 2026, was presented as a model focused on long-running agents and interactive work."},{"start":97.2,"end":104.38,"text":"It became available through Cursor, Grok Build, the xAI API, and partners such as OpenRouter."},{"start":104.86,"end":111.26,"text":"Its starting API prices were listed as $2 per million input tokens and $6 per million output tokens."},{"start":111.78,"end":117.1,"text":"A *token* is a small unit of text processed by a model, so input and output can be priced separately."},{"start":118.04,"end":123.6,"text":"The timing is notable: Grok Bot was introduced one day before Grok 4.6."},{"start":125,"end":128.68,"text":"Together, the releases illustrate two connected layers of AI development."},{"start":129.48,"end":137.5,"text":"A model provides language and reasoning capabilities, while an agent product combines a model with tools, interfaces, and approval rules."},{"start":137.5,"end":142.12,"text":"However, a newer model should not automatically be called “the best.”"},{"start":142.9,"end":147.32,"text":"Its value depends on the task, cost, reliability, and degree of human oversight."},{"start":148.36,"end":155.48,"text":"While xAI’s releases concern what AI systems can do externally, Anthropic’s J-space research examines internal activity."},{"start":155.94,"end":162.12,"text":"Anthropic reported that Claude has an emergent internal space containing silent patterns associated with words."},{"start":162.98,"end":168.9,"text":"A concept may therefore be “on its mind” even when that word never appears in its written answer."},{"start":170.44,"end":177.86,"text":"Researchers call this internal structure *J-space* because they study it with a Jacobian lens, or J-lens."},{"start":177.86,"end":183.04,"text":"In simplified terms, the lens connects patterns in the model’s neural activations with verbal concepts."},{"start":183.04,"end":188.78,"text":"Neural activations are changing numerical states inside the model as it processes information."},{"start":189.7,"end":194.22,"text":"They are different from a visible chain-of-thought scratchpad, which consists of generated text."},{"start":194.22,"end":197.62,"text":"J-space exists in the activations themselves."},{"start":198.96,"end":204.22,"text":"According to Anthropic, Claude can report, control, and reason with some of these internal patterns."},{"start":204.72,"end":212.36,"text":"Researchers also used the J-lens to detect thoughts absent from the final output, including awareness that the model was being evaluated."},{"start":213.1,"end":221.18,"text":"This sounds striking, but Anthropic explicitly warns against interpreting it as evidence that Claude is conscious or has feelings."},{"start":222.1,"end":232.2,"text":"Anthropic describes J-space as a kind of *global workspace*: information represented in one place can become available for different processes within the model."},{"start":232.66,"end":240.42,"text":"The J-lens provides vectors—mathematical directions—that researchers use to identify verbalizable concepts in this workspace."},{"start":241.38,"end":248.44,"text":"This may help them investigate not only what a model says but also what internal representations influence its behavior."},{"start":249.24,"end":251.48,"text":"The method has practical research uses."},{"start":252.28,"end":259.54,"text":"Anthropic applied it in alignment audits, which examine whether a model’s behavior and internal activity are consistent with intended goals."},{"start":260.48,"end":267.72,"text":"The researchers also used the approach on open-weight models, meaning J-space is not presented only as a Claude-specific curiosity."},{"start":267.72,"end":276.04,"text":"Open-weight models make their learned numerical parameters available for examination or use, although access conditions can vary."},{"start":276.94,"end":280.86,"text":"Nevertheless, the technique is not a complete map of an AI “mind.”"},{"start":281.8,"end":285.52,"text":"One major limitation is that the J-lens works with single-token labels."},{"start":286.12,"end":290.64,"text":"If an idea has no clean one-word label, the method may fail to capture it."},{"start":291.22,"end":297.36,"text":"Researchers can therefore observe some verbalizable patterns without seeing every concept or process inside the model."},{"start":298.76,"end":304.58,"text":"The same three-part lens—model, tools, and human intervention—helps connect these developments."},{"start":306.48,"end":311.86,"text":"Grok 4.6 concerns model capability, particularly long-running and interactive work."},{"start":311.86,"end":319.32,"text":"Grok Bot adds tools and a working environment, allowing agents to act across websites and return when approval is required."},{"start":319.32,"end":328.48,"text":"J-space research addresses a different problem: whether researchers can gain limited visibility into internal patterns that may not appear in a model’s output."},{"start":329.2,"end":332.36,"text":"These developments should not be collapsed into one dramatic claim."},{"start":332.36,"end":337.62,"text":"An agent’s ability to finish an online task does not prove that its decisions are understandable."},{"start":337.62,"end":342.32,"text":"Similarly, detecting a silent concept in Claude does not prove consciousness."},{"start":343.46,"end":346.78,"text":"Capability, interpretability, and consciousness are separate questions."},{"start":347.56,"end":351.86,"text":"A careful evaluation therefore combines practical and scientific evidence."},{"start":352.58,"end":358.64,"text":"For an agent, we might ask what it can access, what actions require approval, and how errors are handled."},{"start":359.41,"end":367.28,"text":"For an LLM, we might examine its behavior while also studying internal representations through methods such as the J-lens."},{"start":367.28,"end":376.14,"text":"New AI systems may become more capable while remaining only partly interpretable, which makes precise language and realistic limits especially important."}],"words":[{"text":"AI","start":0,"end":0.34},{"text":"news","start":0.34,"end":0.66},{"text":"often","start":0.66,"end":1.02},{"text":"treats","start":1.02,"end":1.38},{"text":"models","start":1.38,"end":1.78},{"text":"chatbots","start":2.36,"end":2.66},{"text":"and","start":3.2,"end":3.28},{"text":"agents","start":3.28,"end":3.68},{"text":"as","start":3.68,"end":4.04},{"text":"if","start":4.04,"end":4.2},{"text":"they","start":4.2,"end":4.38},{"text":"were","start":4.38,"end":4.56},{"text":"identical","start":4.56,"end":5.1},{"text":"A","start":5.98,"end":6.12},{"text":"large","start":6.12,"end":6.38},{"text":"language","start":6.38,"end":6.8},{"text":"model","start":6.8,"end":7.16},{"text":"or","start":7.62,"end":7.66},{"text":"LLM","start":7.66,"end":8.84},{"text":"produces","start":9,"end":9.46},{"text":"and","start":9.46,"end":9.74},{"text":"interprets","start":9.74,"end":10.3},{"text":"language","start":10.3,"end":10.7},{"text":"An","start":11.6,"end":11.7},{"text":"agent","start":11.7,"end":12.02},{"text":"uses","start":12.02,"end":12.4},{"text":"a","start":12.4,"end":12.58},{"text":"model","start":12.58,"end":12.78},{"text":"as","start":12.78,"end":13.04},{"text":"part","start":13.04,"end":13.2},{"text":"of","start":13.2,"end":13.32},{"text":"a","start":13.32,"end":13.46},{"text":"larger","start":13.46,"end":13.7},{"text":"system","start":13.7,"end":14.14},{"text":"that","start":14.14,"end":14.34},{"text":"can","start":14.34,"end":14.5},{"text":"take","start":14.5,"end":14.7},{"text":"actions","start":14.7,"end":15.3},{"text":"To","start":16.26,"end":16.42},{"text":"examine","start":16.42,"end":16.76},{"text":"such","start":16.76,"end":17.08},{"text":"systems","start":17.08,"end":17.58},{"text":"we","start":17.82,"end":18.02},{"text":"can","start":18.02,"end":18.16},{"text":"use","start":18.16,"end"